      "source_text": "Everyone, Let me start your day with some irony and some food for thought: As you may know, these days it is possible to run decent LLMs locally on your desktop machine. All you need is a decent GPU. Here is what I did recently and what that led to: 1. I installed Ollama and the latest Qwen LLM from Alibaba. Qwen is meant to handle advanced coding tasks, among other things. Installation was smooth and simple. (Don't worry, I created a blocking outbound rule in my firewall.) ;-) 2. As an initial test, I asked Qwen to 'explain race conditions' to me as if I was a C# developer (which I am). I could briefly see some text coming up, and then everything died. My screens went black. No warnings. All dead. In the end, only a full-blown extra hard power reset got my machine up and running again. I haven't had to do that in 5 years. Then I tried the same thing again. Same result. Badly dead machine. =/ =/ =/ =/ =/ =/ =/ =/ =/ =/ =/ =/ =/ =/ =/ =/ The irony was clear (and when asked, Claude agreed): Running an LLM draws *lots* of power. That is why data centers are like black holes for electricity. Running a local LLM on a local GPU is no different. My local GPU needed lots of power very suddenly and the power supply in my local machine may never have experienced such a power spike, even compared to running demanding games. LLMs draw so much power that surrounding societies and systems may suffer. They want all the power you have - and more. Today, I got to experience that first-hand. Take care, everyone! /Björn",
