      "source_text": "Hi everyone! I’ve written a new article about an approach I’ve been using to work with AI agents as a team rather than as isolated autonomous agents. Different agents take on tasks with different levels of complexity, communicate with each other, share findings and build on each other’s work. I thought this approach might be useful for others working on complex AI-driven workflows. Three months ago, I wrote about the challenges that emerged when autonomous AI agents started operating in orchestrator mode: false positives, drift, verification, orchestration and operational stability. Since then, my approach has evolved again. I gradually moved from individual autonomous agents and a centralized orchestrator to agent teams, where agents don’t simply execute tasks in parallel. They share results, warn each other about discovered issues and use previous findings to shape the next experiments. For me, the biggest shift is from: “The agent thinks this will improve performance.” to: “We tested it, measured it, and here is the evidence.” In this article, I’ll break down this approach using a real performance optimization case involving a customer pipeline with around 40 pods, each responsible for a different part of the processing. I’ll show how I split the work between multiple agents, how they communicate findings and hypotheses, how they run experiments in a real test environment, and how the workflow moves from AI-generated recommendations to evidence-based optimization. And this is where performance optimization is particularly interesting: the result is something you can actually see and measure. Here is the baseline solution. Here is the optimized solution. The same data. The same infrastructure. The same workload. And here is the difference in execution time. Instead of saying that an optimization should improve performance, I can actually measure how much it improved it. #ai #ai_automation",
